+
+The five accumulation models share the parameterization used by the RT-only
+families (see `vignette("outliers")`): `ndt` is estimated directly, in seconds,
+and a `poutlier` parameter mixes in an outlier process so that `ndt` is not
+capped at the fastest observed response. The outlier component is a half
+Normal with a fixed scale of 0.2 s, so **reaction times must be in seconds**.
+`cogmod_priors()` and `cogmod_inits()` are worth using rather than
 hand-written priors and `init = 0`: on a log link `init = 0` starts `ndt` at
 `exp(0) = 1` second, above nearly every observed RT, and each of these families
 has at least one direction the likelihood is flat in that `brms` would otherwise
 leave improper.
 
+### Gaussian + Probit (Gaussbit)
+
+The analysis most people run on these data is two separate regressions: a
+**linear model** on the RTs, and a **logistic regression** on the accuracy.
+`cogmod_gaussbit()` is essentially that analysis, done as one model. Its RT
+half is a Gaussian regression - `mu` is the mean RT and `sigma` its SD, exactly
+as in `brms::gaussian()` - and its choice half is a probit regression, `mudec`
+being the probit of the probability of an error. A probit is the logistic
+regression's close cousin: the two curves are nearly the same shape, and
+probit coefficients are about the logit ones divided by 1.7.
+
+What makes it one model rather than two is `rho`, a **trial-level
+correlation** between the RT and the choice. With the errors coded `1`,
+`rho > 0` means the slower a trial, the more likely it is to be an error
+(*slow errors*), and `rho < 0` the reverse (*fast errors*). It changes neither
+half on its own: whatever `rho` is, the RTs are exactly `Normal(mu, sigma)` and
+the error rate exactly `pnorm(mudec)`. `rho` lives on the Fisher-z scale, and
+the correlation itself is `tanh(rho)`.
+
+At `rho = 0` the family *is* the two separate regressions, likelihood and
+priors included, so the two formulas below fit the same model:
+
+```{r}
+#| eval: false
+#| code-fold: false
+
+bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, mudec ~ Condition, rho = 0,
+   family = cogmod_gaussbit())
+
+bf(RT ~ Condition) +
+  bf(Error ~ Condition, family = bernoulli("probit")) +
+  set_rescor(FALSE)
+```
+
+This is what makes it a useful baseline. It knows nothing about the
+decision process: the RT distribution is a symmetric Gaussian with no
+right skew, no non-decision time (the location is free, so a shift *is* `mu`)
+and no outlier component. The difference in `elpd` between it and an
+accumulation model is what the process assumptions buy over the default
+analysis, measured on the same data and the same scale.
+
+Here we let `rho` vary with `Condition`: errors are slower than correct
+responses in the `Accuracy` condition but not in the `Speed` one (see the
+figure above), which is the kind of pattern `rho` is there to pick up.
+
+```{r}
+#| eval: false
+#| code-fold: false
+
+f <- bf(
+  RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition,
+  sigma ~ Condition,
+  mudec ~ Condition,
+  rho ~ Condition,
+  family = cogmod_gaussbit()
+)
+
+m_gaussbit <- brm(f,
+  data = df,
+  prior = cogmod_priors(f, df),
+  init = cogmod_inits(f, df),
+  stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f),
+  chains = 4, iter = 500, backend = "cmdstanr"
+)
+
+m_gaussbit <- brms::add_criterion(m_gaussbit, "loo")  # Add model performance criterion
+```
+```{r}
+#| eval: false
+#| echo: false
+
+saveRDS(m_gaussbit, file = "../models/m_gaussbit.rds")
+```
+
+The coefficients read the way the two separate regressions would. The
+RT half says a mean RT of 0.69 s in `Accuracy` and 0.14 s faster in `Speed`,
+with an SD (`exp()` of `sigma`) of 0.24 s and 0.15 s. The choice half says
+`pnorm(-1.68)` = 4.6% errors in `Accuracy` and `pnorm(-1.68 + 0.39)` = 9.9% in
+`Speed`. `rho` comes out close to zero in both conditions (0.06, 95% CI -0.03
+to 0.14 in `Accuracy`). The errors in `Accuracy` *are* slower, but only by
+about 30 ms, which is small next to an SD of 0.24 s, so a Gaussian barely
+registers it.
+
